          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Haile Selassie</strong><br>\nHaile Selassie was born as Tafari Makonnen on July 23, 1892, in Ejersa Goro, Ethiopia. His father was a chief advisor to Emperor Menelik II. Raised in the royal court, Tafari was seen as a potential heir to the throne from a young age. He received a traditional Ethiopian education as well as instruction in modern subjects and foreign languages, which prepared him for leadership.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nTafari Makonnen became a governor of Harar province after Menelik II's death. He was known for his political savvy and reformist ideas, which gained him popularity. In 1928, he took the title of Ras, meaning \"head,\" an Ethiopian noble rank. By 1930, after the death of Empress Zauditu, Tafari was crowned Emperor Haile Selassie I, \"Power of the Trinity.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modernization Efforts</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Haile Selassie worked towards modernizing Ethiopia. He implemented the country's first constitution in 1931, established a legal system, and attempted to abolish slavery. His efforts also included modernizing the economy and infrastructure, with the construction of roads and the introduction of modern schools and hospitals.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
