          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Italian Invasion and Exile</strong><br>\nIn 1935, Italy invaded Ethiopia, beginning the Second Italo-Ethiopian War. Despite Selassie's plea for League of Nations' intervention, Ethiopia fell to Italian forces in 1936. Haile Selassie went into exile, living in Bath, England, and continuing to advocate for Ethiopia's cause on the international stage.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Liberation</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Haile Selassie obtained British support to fight the Italian occupiers. He returned to Ethiopia in 1941 after the British, with Ethiopian partisans, drove out the Italians. His return marked the restoration of Ethiopia's independence, which was a significant moment in the country's history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-war Reforms</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Haile Selassie continued his efforts to modernize Ethiopia. He played a significant role in forming the Organization of African Unity in 1963, which sought to promote solidarity among African nations. Domestically, he introduced reforms that included a revised constitution, land reform, and steps to reduce feudal power structures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
