          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Conflict and Unrest</strong><br>\nDespite modernization efforts, Haile Selassie's reign faced challenges, including discontent among the military and students. His government was accused of failing to address poverty and famine in the country. The unrest eventually led to demonstrations and opposition against his rule, which weakened his position.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Fall of the Monarchy</strong><br>\nIn 1974, Haile Selassie's government was overthrown by a military coup led by the Derg, a committee of military officers. The emperor was deposed and detained. The Derg abolished the monarchy and established a socialist state, which marked the end of centuries of imperial rule in Ethiopia.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nHaile Selassie died on August 27, 1975, under circumstances that remain controversial; some believe he was murdered on the orders of the Derg regime. He is remembered as a symbol of Ethiopian independence and unity, but also as a figure whose legacy is marked by the contrasts of modernization and authoritarian rule.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
